Root cause

The security group attached to the instance had no inbound rule allowing TCP port 80. Security groups block all inbound traffic until a rule allows it, and they drop blocked packets without any response, so the client sees a timeout rather than an error message. Everything else (instance health, public IP, route to the internet gateway, network ACL) was fine.

Fix

Add an inbound rule that allows TCP port 80 from the client's IP range.

Through Terraform (preferred in this lab, so the code stays the source of truth):

```bash
terraform apply -var="broken=false"
```

Or directly with the AWS CLI:

```bash
aws ec2 authorize-security-group-ingress \
  --group-id <security_group_id> \
  --protocol tcp \
  --port 80 \
  --cidr <your_ip>/32
```

Use your own IP with `/32`, not `0.0.0.0/0`, unless the service is meant to be open to the whole internet. A manual CLI fix is quick, but it creates drift from the Terraform configuration.

Verify the fix:

```bash
curl -v -m 10 http://<public_ip>
```

Expected result: an HTTP 200 response from the web server.

Prevention

- Define security groups in infrastructure as code and review changes in pull requests, so a missing or too-broad rule is caught before it ships.
- Allow only the ports and source ranges a service needs, and reference other security groups instead of IP ranges between application tiers.
- Monitor the endpoint (for example with a Route 53 health check or a CloudWatch Synthetics canary) so the team is alerted before the customer is.
- Use VPC Reachability Analyzer to validate network changes.

Variants to try

Each change below produces a different symptom, so practice telling them apart.

| Change | What you will see | Why |
| Stop the web service on the instance (needs shell access, for example through SSM Session Manager) | Connection refused | Traffic reaches the instance, but nothing is listening on port 80 |
| Allow port 8080 instead of 80 | Timeout | The security group does not allow the port you are using |
| Use the wrong source IP (for example, your ISP changed your address) | Timeout | The source range no longer matches the client |
| Remove the `0.0.0.0/0` route to the internet gateway | Timeout | Replies have no route back to the internet |
| Add a custom network ACL that denies inbound port 80 | Timeout | The network ACL drops traffic before it reaches the security group |

Key takeaways

- Timeout versus refused tells you where to look first.
- Passing instance status checks does not mean the instance is reachable.
- Check in a fixed order so you do not skip a layer: instance state, route, security group, network ACL, then the OS and application.
- Fix the code, not just the console, so the next deploy does not bring the fault back.

Clean up

```bash
terraform destroy
```

Also delete anything you created by hand: the Reachability Analyzer path and analyses, plus any Flow Logs and their CloudWatch log group.

